            <ArticleTitle>Pediatric hypertension: a growing problem.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pop.2014.09.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0095-4543(14)00099-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hypertension in children and adolescents, once thought to be rare, has been estimated at a current prevalence of between 1% and 5% in the United States. The prevalence of primary hypertension continues to increase with the increasing body mass index of the pediatric population. Who is at risk? If and when to screen? When and how to treat? These controversial questions are important to the physician in primary care practice.</AbstractText>
